what All nine ASCII-family scans answer --list by piping their report through sort | head -40, and none of the eleven listings says a row was dropped. The usage line in each header promises name each file and its count; rish_spoken_ascii carries 1,480 files with characters and prints 40 of them. Across the family 2,972 of 3,321 rows are dropped in silence, so a lane asking what is my share reads nothing and cannot tell absence from truncation -- which is how amphora's four spoken characters stood unread. Repair: every listing prints list_shown, list_hidden and list_total after its rows, --list all prints every row, and the usage prose says what the default does. Then seat a meter over the class: a tracked *_scan.sh piping a sort into head -N and printing no census of the drop, gated at zero across the family and reported for the four peer-lane sites (prose_register, socket_dialect, tame_style_long_fn, width_check), which are named rather than swept.
